WEBVTT

NOTE The Closer: Toys R Us

1
00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:29.980
<v Noah Labhart>Hello listeners, I have another special episode to share with you today. I'm sharing another great podcast I've just been made aware of. It's called The Closer from the team at Project Brazen and hosted by financial journalist Aimee Keane. The show focuses on pulling out the high octane emotion and exponential stakes that underscore today's biggest acquisition or investment headlines. In the episode I'm sharing today, the host takes you inside the final days of Toys R Us with Lauren Hirsch,

2
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.580
<v Noah Labhart>the reporter who first revealed the iconic company's impending bankruptcy.

3
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:38.600
<v Noah Labhart>You can expect to hear juicy tidbits around what drove the company out of business.

4
00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:43.840
<v Noah Labhart>Check out The Closer and subscribe today on your favorite podcast catcher.

5
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.480
<v Noah Labhart>And thanks again for listening.

6
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:55.580
<v Guest>If you were a kid at any point in the last, let's say, 60 years or so,

7
00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:59.180
<v Guest>there's a decent chance you remember visiting a Toys R Us store.

8
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.520
<v Noah Labhart>I remember

9
00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.920
<v Guest>being pretty young myself and frankly probably only a few feet high.

10
00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:14.300
<v Guest>So going into Toys R Us felt more like entering this teeming toy palace than it did a big box store.

11
00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.520
<v Guest>There were aisles upon aisles of toys.

12
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.920
<v Guest>There were the Easy Bake Ovens, the Cabbage Patch Dolls, and Barbie everything.

13
00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:29.560
<v Guest>everything. It had to be a special occasion for my parents to bring me and my siblings there in

14
00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:36.060
<v Guest>the first place. But we all knew this one thing, that getting to go into the store usually meant

15
00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:41.140
<v Guest>that if we behaved, we just might walk out with something new. Reporter Lauren Hirsch remembers

16
00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:47.060
<v Guest>something similar. And I remember my mom at the end of the trip to the store saying, I'm so proud

17
00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:52.440
<v Guest>of you. You didn't ask for one toy because I was always a very well-behaved kid. So I just was kind

18
00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:57.880
<v Guest>You know, kind of waiting for my mom to decide which toy she would give me.

19
00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:01.000
<v Guest>But what I honestly remember the most are the ads.

20
00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:04.500
<v Clip>I don't want to grow up on a Toys R Us kid.

21
00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.480
<v Clip>They got a million toys at Toys R Us that I can play with.

22
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:11.000
<v Guest>You know, the famous jingle, I'm a Toys R Us kid at the giraffe.

23
00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:19.140
<v Clip>I don't want to grow up because if I did, I couldn't be a Toys R Us kid.

24
00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:23.680
<v Guest>It wasn't even kind of a question as to whether or not it would ever be around.

25
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:30.100
<v Guest>It was such a permanent part of our existence and, you know, just iconic in terms of its place.

26
00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.960
<v Guest>And in our assumption, it would always be there.

27
00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:35.720
<v Guest>Until it wasn't.

28
00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:37.960
<v Clip>The end of an era.

29
00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:41.880
<v Clip>One of the most recognizable names in American retail is closing its doors today.

30
00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:44.960
<v Clip>John Schumo has more on the end of Toys R Us.

31
00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.560
<v Guest>I tell you, I'll always remember Toys R Us as the store where my children had a complete and total

32
00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.040
<v Clip>meltdown every time we went

33
00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:52.540
<v Guest>there.

34
00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.040
<v Guest>I guess people don't enjoy that anymore.

35
00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:59.640
<v Clip>A disappointing end to what was once the world's biggest toy store.

36
00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:07.940
<v Guest>When Toys R Us closed its doors in the U.S. for good in 2018, it was an emotional day for parents and for kids.

37
00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:12.180
<v Guest>The toy store had beat out all the others for decades, and now it was bankrupt.

38
00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:17.800
<v Guest>Along with its store closures went the chance to experience part of the magic of being a kid.

39
00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:19.820
<v Guest>And it all happened fast.

40
00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:25.100
<v Guest>There was no plan. It was just like chaos and it just like tumbled into bankruptcy.

41
00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.300
<v Guest>So what took down Toys R Us?

42
00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:30.480
<v Guest>As a reporter, you want to have an impact, of course.

43
00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:35.980
<v Guest>But it was a little bit, you know, everyone's like blaming me for killing Toys R Us.

44
00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:41.800
<v Guest>That story begins much earlier and it involves one of the most notable leveraged buyouts in retail history.

45
00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:46.140
<v Guest>That was an odd experience, I would say.

46
00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:49.020
<v Guest>We'll be right back with the story.

47
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.280
<v Guest>Welcome back to The Closer.

48
00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.980
<v Guest>My guest this episode is journalist Lauren Hirsch.

49
00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.880
<v Guest>I'm a business reporter at The New York Times.

50
00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:04.420
<v Guest>And while Toys R Us was going bankrupt, I covered this story at CNBC.

51
00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:10.180
<v Guest>The story of Toys R Us begins all the way back at the end of the Second World War with one man.

52
00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:16.840
<v Guest>is known as, you know, one of the countries, maybe the world's greatest merchants.

53
00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:20.540
<v Guest>And he knew how to appeal to his ideal customers.

54
00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:25.100
<v Guest>Over the years, I have tried to teach children to say, I need

55
00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:26.040
<v Noah Labhart>it rather than I

56
00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:26.540
<v Guest>want it.

57
00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:31.880
<v Guest>Nobody really has to buy the product. So it's kind of a happy consumer.

58
00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.760
<v Guest>Lazarus's first foray into retail started in 1948 in Washington, D.C.

59
00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:41.340
<v Guest>A lot of his friends were having children. It was the post-war baby boom.

60
00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.820
<v Guest>he decided to open up a crib and baby furniture store.

61
00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.660
<v Guest>But he quickly moved on from baby gear.

62
00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.460
<v Guest>This was stuff parents usually only bought once.

63
00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:52.560
<v Guest>And he got into the toy business.

64
00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:58.200
<v Guest>He knew that children tire of their toys and their stuffed animals really quickly.

65
00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:01.220
<v Guest>So if you build a store devoted to toys,

66
00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:04.060
<v Guest>you're going to get kids and obviously therefore parents

67
00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:08.300
<v Guest>coming in frequently to constantly replenish their supplies.

68
00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:10.580
<v Guest>So he created Toys R Us.

69
00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.380
<v Guest>The R in Toys R Us, it's backwards.

70
00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:19.960
<v Guest>And that's because he wanted to mimic what it would look like if a child was writing Toys R Us.

71
00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:23.220
<v Guest>You know, that kind of childhood lore, which he really, he understood.

72
00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:27.960
<v Guest>And the people who sold products into Toys R Us, they just loved him.

73
00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:33.700
<v Guest>I'm imagining an almost Walt Disney-like ability to tap into, like, everybody's inner child.

74
00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.060
<v Guest>It's a total Walt Disney thing.

75
00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:39.100
<v Guest>And, right, listen, like, Walt Disney also was very, very rich.

76
00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:45.540
<v Guest>You know, it was a, you know, a wonderful combination of understanding what children want.

77
00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:48.560
<v Guest>And yes, you know, understanding how to monetizing it.

78
00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:57.580
<v Guest>And he was both a great businessman with a very keen sense of the kind of store that he needed to create to, you know, feed the children.

79
00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:05.300
<v Guest>A big part of this success came not only from understanding his customers, but also from fostering strong ties with the toy makers.

80
00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:13.140
<v Guest>Toys R Us is kind of the most special thing about it was its relationship with the toy companies.

81
00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:16.320
<v Guest>And so what he was able to do was he got exclusives.

82
00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:20.920
<v Guest>He got toy companies to promise they would only sell certain products in its stores.

83
00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:26.180
<v Guest>And that kind of made it such that if you wanted a certain toy, you had to go to Toys R Us.

84
00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:32.280
<v Guest>So he took these really big box stores, these large chunks of real estate, and grew them across the country.

85
00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:38.640
<v Guest>And so all of a sudden, Toys R Us became this omnipresent experience throughout the country.

86
00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:42.580
<v Guest>And if you wanted a toy, you knew the first place that you had to go was Toys R Us.

87
00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:50.880
<v Guest>Lazarus steered the company through a decades-long expansion, at home and abroad.

88
00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:54.280
<v Guest>It was operating in 35 countries at its peak.

89
00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:57.920
<v Guest>He launched a sister brand, Babies R Us, took the company public.

90
00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:04.180
<v Guest>And by the early 90s, he created a proper toy empire that beat out just about any other toy retailer.

91
00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:05.940
<v Guest>It was the category killer.

92
00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:11.160
<v Guest>By 1994, though, Lazarus was in his 70s and ready to retire.

93
00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:15.360
<v Guest>He stayed on the board as chairman for four more years, and his successor was picked.

94
00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:24.140
<v Guest>There was no immediate CEO that followed him that had the same kind of relationships, the same kind of, you know, understanding of the business.

95
00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:29.060
<v Guest>business. The business had been Lazarus's imagination brought to life. Even an experienced

96
00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:35.240
<v Guest>CEO couldn't quite replicate that. The industry all of a sudden started to really change. Obviously,

97
00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:41.460
<v Guest>the first thing we think of is e-commerce. You had the dot-com boom. The dot-com boom put a lot

98
00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:46.260
<v Guest>of pressure on stores like Toys R Us to adapt their in-store offerings for an online crowd.

99
00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:51.860
<v Guest>So in response, in 2000, Toys R Us signed a first-of-its-kind deal with Amazon to sell toys

100
00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:57.300
<v Guest>always online. But the relationship quickly dissolved over a difference of opinion on just

101
00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:02.840
<v Guest>how exclusive the arrangement was. And it ended in a lengthy and expensive legal battle.

102
00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:08.380
<v Guest>So not only did that deal go awry, it also became a huge distraction for the company.

103
00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:14.040
<v Guest>It was a distraction from an other equally big threat that had been growing since the mid-90s,

104
00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:16.780
<v Guest>the rise of big box stores like Walmart and Target.

105
00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:25.900
<v Guest>Their big strength was their ability to sell products on the cheap. And so all of a sudden, they were able to fight Toys R Us on the price of its toys.

106
00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:40.100
<v Guest>Yeah, Lauren, I'm imagining, you know, Saturday morning, a parent's got errands to run. They've promised little whomever that he or she could pick out a toy. But that parent could also pick up toilet paper, maybe some groceries, if they did it all at Walmart.

107
00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:49.980
<v Guest>So what Walmart was super smart on is it knew that if it could use a toy to get you into its store, it could get you to shop for a million other things.

108
00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:59.980
<v Guest>And what Toys R Us was supposed to do in response to that learning from Walmart was make its store a place where you still had to go because it's this wonderful, magical experience.

109
00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:27.060
<v Guest>But the problem was Toys R Us had gotten so big and so powerful and it really hadn't needed to invest in its stores in that way to become so big and powerful that it didn't really realize times had changed. It just never reacted. And so therefore, it never really gave shoppers sufficient reason to go there versus Walmart, where not only could you pick up five pieces of products, but the toy is probably going to be cheaper.

110
00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:38.180
<v Guest>And in resting on the strengths of what the Toys R Us brand once was, the business lost sight of its customers, both the toy makers and the people coming into the store to buy a toy.

111
00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:44.840
<v Guest>First, it just didn't have those same kind of relationships that Lazarus worked really hard to foster.

112
00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:49.720
<v Guest>You know, those relationships are dinners, they're calls, they're, you know, how's your son doing?

113
00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:55.640
<v Guest>It didn't have that. And it didn't invest. It took its customers for granted.

114
00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:02.160
<v Guest>frankly, is it had a lot of men in management. Its prime customers were women shopping for their kids.

115
00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:06.860
<v Guest>And I would speak to former employees who would say, you know, they kind of didn't get it.

116
00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:11.980
<v Guest>All of this added up to years of grim earnings. And by the early 2000s,

117
00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:14.280
<v Guest>investors on Wall Street were souring on the company.

118
00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:19.480
<v Guest>Its stock had dropped. You know, investors were worried about its competition that it was seeing

119
00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:26.220
<v Guest>from the big box stores. They weren't convinced that it really had kind of a strong future ahead.

120
00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:33.780
<v Guest>frankly flailing. It had lost its way. It was still this iconic store. It was still omnipresent,

121
00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:38.940
<v Guest>but it wasn't as, you know, almost seductive as it had once been. And that became a real

122
00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:43.920
<v Guest>challenge for the store. A challenge for Toys R Us, but it was an opportunity for a trio of

123
00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:50.160
<v Guest>investors. In 2005, three firms, KKR, Bain Capital, and the real estate investment trust company

124
00:10:54.980 --> 00:11:00.540
<v Guest>There was almost kind of a ritualistic game plan that private equity firms did when it came to retailers.

125
00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:08.540
<v Guest>One was, back in those days, the only way a retailer can grow is buy more stores.

126
00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:15.040
<v Guest>So you buy a company with a great brand and you say, great, a really easy way to grow sales is to simply build more stores.

127
00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:16.800
<v Guest>So that's plan number one.

128
00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:24.080
<v Guest>Plan number two is there is real estate, and real estate can be very valuable.

129
00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:27.700
<v Guest>At the time, the real estate industry was very different.

130
00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:33.040
<v Guest>Malls were considered to be a valuable place for real estate because people still went to malls.

131
00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:40.540
<v Guest>So the two private equity firms in Enfrenado, they wanted to grow the sales, monetize real estate,

132
00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:45.640
<v Guest>and yes, do what every private equity firm does, which is slash costs all that it can,

133
00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:48.700
<v Guest>and reap the rewards on the other side.

134
00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:51.620
<v Guest>Another feature of these deals?

135
00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:52.580
<v Guest>Debt.

136
00:11:52.840 --> 00:11:54.360
<v Guest>Often lots of debt.

137
00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:57.180
<v Guest>Private equity investors will finance the takeover

138
00:11:57.180 --> 00:12:00.660
<v Guest>by raising debt secured by the assets of the company they're buying.

139
00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:03.240
<v Guest>But instead of assuming that debt themselves,

140
00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:05.580
<v Guest>the target company will hold that liability.

141
00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:09.160
<v Guest>The idea is that as you turn around the struggling company,

142
00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:10.940
<v Guest>the company pays down the debt,

143
00:12:11.060 --> 00:12:13.520
<v Guest>and then you, the investor, reap the upside

144
00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:15.800
<v Guest>when you either sell the company or take it public.

145
00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:23.580
<v Guest>The downside, though, is that the cost of servicing that debt is often an expensive burden for the company trying to right itself.

146
00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:31.680
<v Guest>And in the case of Toys R Us, KKR, Bain, and Vornado added about $5 billion in debt to the company through this takeover.

147
00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:38.720
<v Guest>So instead of investing in e-commerce or improving the in-store experience, Toys R Us now had to pay down this debt.

148
00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:49.980
<v Guest>I actually spoke to someone who quit shortly after the deal was signed because he was like, there's just no way a company is going to be able to survive this much debt on this profitability.

149
00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:54.100
<v Guest>This debt became effectively an albatross on the company.

150
00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:59.940
<v Guest>It couldn't make any of the investments that it needed to do to respond to the big box retailers like Walmart.

151
00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:01.240
<v Guest>It couldn't respond to Amazon.

152
00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:07.120
<v Guest>on. Right. And that was because any available cash that the company did have, most of that

153
00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:11.860
<v Guest>available cash was going to servicing that debt, which I think at one point was a cost of about

154
00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:17.400
<v Guest>$400 million a year. Yeah. And the problem is it becomes this, you know, you're just circling the

155
00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:21.840
<v Guest>drain, right? You're just trying to survive enough to pay the debt. You don't have any

156
00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:28.120
<v Guest>capacity to think about what it means to actually grow. Meanwhile, the kids the business depended

157
00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:34.700
<v Guest>it on. The kids Charles Lazarus had convinced needed toys. They were also changing. So there

158
00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:40.200
<v Guest>was this really fascinating dynamic where effectively kids became older, younger. That

159
00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:46.220
<v Guest>sounds odd, but what that means is kids grew out of toys faster. There were video games,

160
00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:52.240
<v Guest>there were other things to do. And so that frankly disrupted like the business model itself. Your

161
00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:55.240
<v Guest>your core customer is aging out much faster.

162
00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:58.020
<v Guest>It was an existential moment for the business.

163
00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:00.640
<v Guest>How does it respond to this generation of kids?

164
00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:04.480
<v Guest>How does it better serve their parents or the people shopping for them online?

165
00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:10.020
<v Guest>And how does it do all of this while paying down hundreds of millions of dollars of debt each year?

166
00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:14.500
<v Guest>The next move was for the investors to try to put the company back on the public market.

167
00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:16.360
<v Guest>They were going to go public in 2010.

168
00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:20.700
<v Guest>You know, it didn't look like they were going to get the valuation that they needed or wanted.

169
00:14:22.240 --> 00:14:30.640
<v Guest>for IPOs. And then it kind of infamously just floated out there. They never really officially

170
00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:35.700
<v Guest>said what the plan was going to be after it didn't go public. The private equity firms didn't really

171
00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:41.220
<v Guest>talk about it a lot publicly. It was just kind of there. And it was so big. And ultimately,

172
00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:46.680
<v Guest>what they did do was they wrote it down to zero. Basically, Toys R Us kind of withered

173
00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:50.820
<v Guest>under their ownership because they knew they weren't going to make money off of it.

174
00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:54.560
<v Guest>They knew they weren't going to be able to take it public.

175
00:14:54.760 --> 00:14:56.280
<v Guest>There was no one to sell it to.

176
00:14:56.520 --> 00:15:01.560
<v Guest>So it just faded out of sight while its value deteriorated.

177
00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:06.900
<v Guest>Toys R Us was a write-off for the firms, but it was still a company.

178
00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:09.580
<v Guest>It had real operations, real debt to be paid.

179
00:15:09.880 --> 00:15:11.680
<v Guest>So fast forward a few years.

180
00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:16.580
<v Guest>Around 2017 was when retail really dropped off a cliff.

181
00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:23.020
<v Guest>All of those retail deals in the mid-2000s by private equity firms were turning sour.

182
00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:28.660
<v Guest>They were going bankrupt because the industry had changed and these companies never reacted

183
00:15:28.660 --> 00:15:30.760
<v Guest>and they had a lot of debt and they had to go under.

184
00:15:31.060 --> 00:15:37.540
<v Guest>So Toys R Us' owners knew that and they were trying to restructure the business.

185
00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:42.260
<v Guest>And basically when you're structuring a debt, you go to lenders and you say, can we just

186
00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:44.240
<v Guest>restrike this deal a little bit?

187
00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:51.480
<v Guest>It might, you know, it might be a little bit of a haircut for you, but it's better for all of us to do that rather than let it go bankrupt.

188
00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:52.560
<v Guest>No one wants that.

189
00:15:56.220 --> 00:16:06.100
<v Guest>The problem was they were unable to get lenders to work with them on a kind of deal and a kind of deal to restructure.

190
00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:09.760
<v Guest>And they realized, oh, like, we're going to have to file for bankruptcy.

191
00:16:10.820 --> 00:16:17.840
<v Guest>Now, filing for bankruptcy is one of those things that sounds terrible and obviously it's not great, but it's not always a death knell for a company.

192
00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:21.280
<v Guest>You know, Macy's filed for bankruptcy, merged.

193
00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:23.000
<v Guest>We all still shop at Macy's.

194
00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:25.440
<v Guest>So that doesn't have to be the end.

195
00:16:27.260 --> 00:16:31.640
<v Guest>So, yes, I caught wind of the fact that they were considering bankruptcy.

196
00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:35.660
<v Guest>And it just wasn't on anyone's radar screen.

197
00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:38.100
<v Guest>And people weren't expecting it.

198
00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:45.240
<v Guest>why that's a problem is those who didn't expect it included the suppliers, included the toy

199
00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:51.820
<v Guest>companies that sold into Toys R Us. So I find out that Toys R Us is weighing bankruptcy. I'm

200
00:16:51.820 --> 00:16:54.720
<v Guest>pretty surprised because, as I said, it wasn't on my radar screen.

201
00:16:56.880 --> 00:17:00.560
<v Guest>Lauren had just left a job at Reuters and was about to start at CNBC.

202
00:17:00.860 --> 00:17:04.640
<v Guest>But between jobs, she gets this tip. So she calls up her new editor.

203
00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:32.720
<v Guest>And I was like, I don't really know you that well. Super excited to start my new job. Just FYI, like I have this tip. What do I do? He was just like, don't do any because I couldn't write it yet. So he was like, just like sit on it. And it was so horrible because, you know, you're as a reporter, like you don't want to lose a story. So I remember like doing a cycling class, just like thinking about Toys R Us, being like, oh, I hope no one gets the story. I hope no one gets the story.

204
00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:38.460
<v Guest>Also couldn't like, you know, I had it, but I didn't like fully have it because I didn't want to chase it too much.

205
00:17:38.460 --> 00:17:45.280
<v Guest>And like to let other reporters know what I was, you know, because sometimes when you're chasing a story, other people find out that you're chasing and it spreads.

206
00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:47.860
<v Guest>Then her first day on the new job comes around.

207
00:17:48.340 --> 00:17:49.800
<v Guest>Our office was in New Jersey.

208
00:17:49.880 --> 00:17:59.520
<v Guest>And I remember getting off the shuttle in New Jersey and calling another source to confirm the story and be like, hey, it's my first day.

209
00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:00.220
<v Guest>It's the CNBC.

210
00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:01.600
<v Guest>Just FYI.

211
00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:03.120
<v Guest>Like, I'm hearing this.

212
00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:08.480
<v Guest>wrote the story, I like bought a new computer. I think I need a new computer anyway. I didn't just

213
00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:13.060
<v Guest>buy it for this story. But like I like had the story ready to go like in my week in between

214
00:18:13.060 --> 00:18:22.640
<v Guest>jobs. So I like an editor's dream. I'm very well behaved. Yes, very well behaved. So anyway,

215
00:18:22.760 --> 00:18:29.920
<v Guest>I think I may have ended up running it my second day at the job. So I ran it and it was just like

216
00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:31.580
<v Guest>like an odd experience.

217
00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:32.900
<v Guest>Just, you know, it was odd,

218
00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:35.660
<v Guest>just like breaking the story at this new job.

219
00:18:36.060 --> 00:18:36.560
<v Guest>The

220
00:18:36.380 --> 00:18:38.120
<v Noah Labhart>biggest toy store chain in the world.

221
00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:41.000
<v Guest>I'm a Toys

222
00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:41.500
<v Noah Labhart>R Us kid.

223
00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:42.260
<v Noah Labhart>Now

224
00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:43.120
<v Guest>facing a very

225
00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:43.620
<v Noah Labhart>grown up problem.

226
00:18:44.540 --> 00:18:45.980
<v Noah Labhart>The largest toy store

227
00:18:45.980 --> 00:18:46.940
<v Guest>chain in the U.S.

228
00:18:47.100 --> 00:18:49.420
<v Guest>Toys R Us has filed for bankruptcy protection.

229
00:18:49.820 --> 00:18:51.540
<v Guest>The Chapter 11 filing overnight

230
00:18:51.540 --> 00:18:52.880
<v Noah Labhart>is among the largest ever

231
00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:54.280
<v Guest>by a specialty retailer.

232
00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:56.660
<v Guest>Now they remain open,

233
00:18:56.660 --> 00:18:58.160
<v Guest>But 64,000

234
00:18:58.160 --> 00:19:01.080
<v Noah Labhart>employees at 1,600 stores may wonder

235
00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:03.920
<v Guest>whether the exit sign could at some point be for them.

236
00:19:04.360 --> 00:19:08.000
<v Guest>And it was the craziest reaction I have ever had to a story.

237
00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:10.620
<v Guest>We'll be back with Lauren Hirsch after the break.

238
00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:19.760
<v Guest>In 2017, Lauren Hirsch was taking a bit of time off between reporting jobs when she got a tip.

239
00:19:20.100 --> 00:19:22.460
<v Guest>Toys R Us was thinking about filing for bankruptcy.

240
00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:27.200
<v Guest>bankruptcy. The story was hers to break, and it would happen on her first day on the job in the

241
00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:37.320
<v Guest>CNBC newsroom. You know, usually when you write about one company acquiring another company,

242
00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:40.900
<v Guest>you know, a stock might move. And maybe if you write about a potential bankruptcy,

243
00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:45.500
<v Guest>the bonds will take a hit. But like the bonds just dropped, like dropped massively.

244
00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:51.880
<v Guest>And there was shock, like no one believed it. I had people calling me like, this isn't true,

245
00:19:52.460 --> 00:19:53.060
<v Guest>This is wrong.

246
00:19:54.580 --> 00:20:08.920
<v Guest>And all of a sudden, all of the toy companies who had no idea this company was struggling were like, well, I am not going to give you my toys because if you go bankrupt, I don't get my money.

247
00:20:09.380 --> 00:20:17.780
<v Guest>And so all of a sudden, the next three weeks were this like complete frenzy where it was effectively a run on the bank.

248
00:20:17.980 --> 00:20:21.940
<v Guest>No toys would ship their products to Toys R Us.

249
00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:26.840
<v Guest>terrified that it was going to go bankrupt. And again, this kind of goes back to this idea that

250
00:20:26.840 --> 00:20:30.620
<v Guest>they didn't have those relationships with the suppliers, with the toys companies. So

251
00:20:30.620 --> 00:20:36.780
<v Guest>there wasn't that trust. The plan had been to file after the holiday season,

252
00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:41.740
<v Guest>which is always what you want to do because the holiday season is like the most important time

253
00:20:41.740 --> 00:20:45.900
<v Guest>for a retailer. It's when you make the majority of your sales. That's definitely true for Toys R Us.

254
00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:56.040
<v Guest>But because, frankly, I broke the story and I unintentionally caused a run on the bank, it filed for bankruptcy in three weeks.

255
00:20:59.180 --> 00:21:03.320
<v Guest>So, you know, usually when you file for bankruptcy, you want to have a plan to emerge.

256
00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:08.860
<v Guest>You kind of work with your creditors like, all right, like we're going to go into bankruptcy and we're going to do A, B and C.

257
00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:10.880
<v Guest>And this is how we'll get out of bankruptcy.

258
00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:11.960
<v Guest>Like there was no plan.

259
00:21:12.100 --> 00:21:15.560
<v Guest>It was just like chaos and it just like tumbled into bankruptcy.

260
00:21:17.560 --> 00:21:20.080
<v Guest>As a reporter, you want to have an impact, of course.

261
00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:24.760
<v Guest>But it was a little bit, you know, everyone's like blaming me for killing Toys R Us.

262
00:21:24.820 --> 00:21:29.060
<v Guest>So that was an odd experience, I would say.

263
00:21:29.460 --> 00:21:32.000
<v Guest>Can we just stay for a minute on how you felt at the time?

264
00:21:32.440 --> 00:21:37.420
<v Guest>Well, I was, I mean, I was very stressed because I felt really bad.

265
00:21:37.460 --> 00:21:41.340
<v Guest>I mean, I shouldn't have felt terrible because I did my job, you know.

266
00:21:41.340 --> 00:21:46.100
<v Guest>And frankly, the public should know, like people should know when a company is weighing restructuring.

267
00:21:46.180 --> 00:21:47.420
<v Guest>So I didn't do anything wrong.

268
00:21:47.720 --> 00:21:50.660
<v Guest>But it was a very odd experience.

269
00:21:50.820 --> 00:21:53.120
<v Guest>I had never really been a part of this story.

270
00:21:53.180 --> 00:21:55.440
<v Guest>And all of a sudden I become a part of this story.

271
00:21:55.600 --> 00:22:01.560
<v Guest>And that's an uncomfortable position for many journalists because you're just trying to kind of let people know what's happening.

272
00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:06.420
<v Guest>You're not necessarily trying to be a part of the thing that's happening.

273
00:22:07.040 --> 00:22:12.240
<v Guest>So you break the story that Toys R Us is considering restructuring, doing restructuring through bankruptcy.

274
00:22:12.620 --> 00:22:14.180
<v Guest>They're going to do it after the holiday season.

275
00:22:14.480 --> 00:22:18.860
<v Guest>Your story comes out and they actually end up in bankruptcy court in just three weeks.

276
00:22:19.100 --> 00:22:20.880
<v Guest>How did this go for the company?

277
00:22:22.560 --> 00:22:27.620
<v Guest>Yes. So every time a company goes bankrupt, its lawyers go in and they tell the story.

278
00:22:28.140 --> 00:22:30.180
<v Guest>How did we get here? Who are we?

279
00:22:30.800 --> 00:22:33.420
<v Guest>And at the same time, right, you're kind of making a pitch.

280
00:22:33.420 --> 00:22:41.660
<v Guest>You're making a pitch to your creditors. You're making a pitch to the bankruptcy judge who ultimately has to decide whether or not he or she will let you emerge from court.

281
00:22:42.200 --> 00:22:51.220
<v Guest>And what the lawyers did was they really, you know, they appealed to people's memories of the stories and the nostalgia factor.

282
00:22:51.420 --> 00:22:56.020
<v Guest>So they got there and they literally sang the Toys R Us jingle.

283
00:22:56.300 --> 00:22:57.860
<v Guest>The lawyers sang the jingle.

284
00:22:58.040 --> 00:22:59.760
<v Guest>Yes, they sang the jingle.

285
00:23:08.380 --> 00:23:13.740
<v Guest>It was working with Kirkland &amp; Ellis, who is, you know, one of the top, if not the top, bankruptcy law firms.

286
00:23:13.760 --> 00:23:19.060
<v Guest>So they're an excellent law firm, but I think some people were just a little bit concerned.

287
00:23:20.140 --> 00:23:22.080
<v Guest>Jingle isn't going to get you out of this problem.

288
00:23:22.240 --> 00:23:27.680
<v Guest>And honestly, I think that even its approach to that first day of, you know, it's fine.

289
00:23:27.740 --> 00:23:29.700
<v Guest>We're Toys R Us. Like, let's sing you the song.

290
00:23:30.380 --> 00:23:31.360
<v Guest>It wasn't enough.

291
00:23:31.640 --> 00:23:33.940
<v Guest>Like, you know, that was kind of like the story of Toys R Us.

292
00:23:34.040 --> 00:23:35.520
<v Guest>Like, that's how it got into that place.

293
00:23:35.640 --> 00:23:37.220
<v Guest>Like, no, it wasn't fine.

294
00:23:37.360 --> 00:23:38.460
<v Guest>Like, they had too much debt.

295
00:23:38.600 --> 00:23:39.640
<v Guest>They couldn't pay it.

296
00:23:39.680 --> 00:23:40.980
<v Guest>Like, they were in bankruptcy court.

297
00:23:44.540 --> 00:23:49.960
<v Guest>So outside of appealing to everyone's nostalgia for the Toys R Us brand, what was the company's strategy?

298
00:23:50.060 --> 00:23:50.820
<v Guest>What was the plan?

299
00:23:51.280 --> 00:23:55.740
<v Guest>The plan almost in every retail bankruptcy is emerge leaner.

300
00:23:55.760 --> 00:23:57.880
<v Guest>So you close the stores that are unprofitable.

301
00:23:57.920 --> 00:23:59.200
<v Guest>You hopefully get rid of the debt.

302
00:23:59.700 --> 00:24:03.380
<v Guest>Maybe you sell off certain divisions that aren't making money.

303
00:24:04.300 --> 00:24:11.260
<v Guest>But to do any of that, Toys R Us needed the support of its creditors.

304
00:24:11.280 --> 00:24:13.900
<v Guest>So it needed to have an amazing holiday season.

305
00:24:13.980 --> 00:24:21.460
<v Guest>It was the most important holiday season of its life because it needed to keep its creditors at bay.

306
00:24:21.620 --> 00:24:26.820
<v Guest>Come out on the other side, just a little bit leaner or maybe a lot of it leaner, but stronger.

307
00:24:27.460 --> 00:24:29.040
<v Guest>So that was the plan.

308
00:24:29.740 --> 00:24:34.780
<v Guest>happened after that holiday season? You know, upon reflection, it was actually pretty obvious,

309
00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:40.040
<v Guest>I think, to a lot of people what was going to happen, which was Target, Walmart, they had been

310
00:24:40.040 --> 00:24:46.660
<v Guest>vying for Toys R Us' business for a while. Toys may not make a ton of money, but they're a huge

311
00:24:46.660 --> 00:24:53.500
<v Guest>tool to bring people into your stores. So what they did was they just slashed their prices.

312
00:24:55.160 --> 00:24:57.980
<v Guest>They made toys as cheap as possible.

313
00:24:58.400 --> 00:25:01.120
<v Guest>And, you know, I'm sure no Walmart target would ever say this explicitly,

314
00:25:01.320 --> 00:25:06.660
<v Guest>but what it appeared to be was, you know, let's put a nail in this coffin.

315
00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:08.640
<v Guest>This thing is on the floor.

316
00:25:08.860 --> 00:25:10.960
<v Guest>Let's just give it one more kick and see what happens.

317
00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:14.720
<v Guest>And it was very hard for them to compete with those prices.

318
00:25:14.820 --> 00:25:18.160
<v Guest>It was a struggling retailer that had never invested in itself.

319
00:25:18.780 --> 00:25:22.940
<v Guest>So it was the most important holiday season of its life.

320
00:25:23.580 --> 00:25:27.520
<v Guest>And it ultimately ended up being its last holiday season of its life.

321
00:25:29.400 --> 00:25:33.480
<v Guest>The company calculated it would run out of cash just a few months later.

322
00:25:34.080 --> 00:25:36.540
<v Guest>It was, you know, it was just exceptionally grim.

323
00:25:37.040 --> 00:25:42.940
<v Guest>At what point did the company realize it wasn't likely to come out of bankruptcy, that it would have to close for good?

324
00:25:43.800 --> 00:25:47.100
<v Guest>They, like anyone, wanted to come out of this the other side.

325
00:25:47.200 --> 00:25:52.620
<v Guest>But they had creditors who basically believed this company was worth more dead than alive.

326
00:25:53.760 --> 00:26:00.060
<v Guest>And bankruptcy can be a powerful tool to get rid of debt, to get rid of real estate.

327
00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:12.040
<v Guest>But it's also a very vulnerable position because you are now negotiating with creditors who, you know, like it or not, it's capitalism.

328
00:26:12.160 --> 00:26:13.140
<v Guest>They're in there to make money.

329
00:26:13.220 --> 00:26:15.720
<v Guest>They're not in there because they care about the future of Toys R Us.

330
00:26:16.740 --> 00:26:20.300
<v Guest>And the creditors thought, you know, kill it, sell it for pieces.

331
00:26:20.740 --> 00:26:24.460
<v Guest>And they realized, all right, we have to liquidate.

332
00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:27.780
<v Guest>Like, this thing is never going to come out of bankruptcy court.

333
00:26:28.140 --> 00:26:31.200
<v Guest>The company announced plans to liquidate in March of 2018.

334
00:26:31.480 --> 00:26:34.000
<v Guest>And by June, it had closed its U.S. stores.

335
00:26:34.760 --> 00:26:35.920
<v Guest>How did the staff react?

336
00:26:36.400 --> 00:26:40.640
<v Guest>It was an incredibly stressful time for employees, you know, as one can imagine.

337
00:26:40.860 --> 00:26:43.380
<v Guest>You know, you don't know what the next day is going to bring.

338
00:26:43.760 --> 00:26:47.320
<v Guest>The idea of working for a bankruptcy company is really hard.

339
00:26:47.320 --> 00:26:53.620
<v Guest>You know, I remember talking to a source close with Toys R Us saying, you know, there's a lot of frustration with you.

340
00:26:53.680 --> 00:26:58.040
<v Guest>Like the creditors are mad because they feel like you're not giving them updated information.

341
00:26:58.360 --> 00:27:00.460
<v Guest>The employees feel like you're not being straight with them.

342
00:27:00.940 --> 00:27:06.020
<v Guest>And my source was like, well, sure, but we have no idea what's going on.

343
00:27:06.120 --> 00:27:12.720
<v Guest>And, you know, I talk a lot about how Toys R Us had kind of lost the thing that made it special.

344
00:27:12.920 --> 00:27:13.800
<v Guest>And that is true.

345
00:27:14.420 --> 00:27:30.820
<v Guest>But there were a number of employees, a number that I spoke to that still really loved Toys R Us. And when it ultimately went bankrupt and when it ultimately liquidated, they were devastated. Of course, it's upsetting to lose your job, but there was also an emotional pain.

346
00:27:30.820 --> 00:27:39.400
<v Guest>there was literally a Facebook page, the Dead Giraffe Society, where employees kind of, you know, had their memories.

347
00:27:39.500 --> 00:27:42.300
<v Guest>I was invited. I then disinvited. And I understand why.

348
00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:49.560
<v Guest>The day the Toys R Us, yeah, the day the Toys R Us finally closed its door, one of the employees was like, come, like, we're sharing our Toys R Us memories.

349
00:27:49.840 --> 00:27:52.740
<v Guest>And then she was like, well, maybe like they don't want to see you.

350
00:27:52.740 --> 00:27:53.360
<v Guest>I was like, I understand.

351
00:27:55.600 --> 00:28:00.000
<v Guest>So it was it was hard and upsetting and, you know, tragic.

352
00:28:00.820 --> 00:28:05.560
<v Guest>lost their jobs. And that's always a terrible situation. Of all the deals that you cover,

353
00:28:05.860 --> 00:28:11.200
<v Guest>looking back on this one, why is it one of your favorites? As a reporter, you can sometimes

354
00:28:11.740 --> 00:28:16.140
<v Guest>operate a little bit in a bubble where you're talking to sources and you're excited about your

355
00:28:16.140 --> 00:28:21.780
<v Guest>story, but like you don't know how much the broader world really values it or is paying

356
00:28:21.780 --> 00:28:27.820
<v Guest>attention. And everyone was paying attention to Toys R Us. Everyone loves Toys R Us. Everyone had

357
00:28:27.820 --> 00:28:34.680
<v Guest>had a Toys R Us memory. Everyone was sad that it went away. And obviously, it's sad that it went

358
00:28:34.680 --> 00:28:40.020
<v Guest>away. So I don't celebrate that. But covering and being part of a story that like touched so many

359
00:28:40.020 --> 00:28:46.920
<v Guest>lives, and honestly did, was a very special experience because you just you realize covering

360
00:28:46.920 --> 00:28:51.760
<v Guest>bankruptcy and covering M&amp;A, a lot of it is just like covering money moving. And you realize kind

361
00:28:51.760 --> 00:28:57.640
<v Guest>of the human impact of all of it. And that was a really just gratifying experience for me.

362
00:28:57.820 --> 00:29:03.180
<v Guest>Mm-hmm. The brand itself or the company has, at least as I've been able to see,

363
00:29:03.240 --> 00:29:09.200
<v Guest>has tried to make a few different comebacks. What is the Toys R Us business that exists today? Like,

364
00:29:09.200 --> 00:29:14.740
<v Guest>to be quite literal about it, does it have employees? Who is in charge? What is Toys R Us

365
00:29:14.740 --> 00:29:19.800
<v Guest>today? They've sold the brand, I think they sold it to the creditors actually, and I believe it

366
00:29:19.800 --> 00:29:25.820
<v Guest>may have changed hands one more time after that. You know, it's nothing. I hate to say that. It's

367
00:29:27.820 --> 00:29:34.280
<v Guest>like a pop-up in Macy's, but it's not, it will never be what it was. I mean, I still get emails

368
00:29:34.280 --> 00:29:38.720
<v Guest>like Toys R Us is coming back. And I think, yes, there are people who own the Toys R Us brand who

369
00:29:38.720 --> 00:29:44.000
<v Guest>are trying to monetize it. But unfortunately, I don't think Toys R Us, as we knew it as children,

370
00:29:44.120 --> 00:29:50.100
<v Guest>is ever coming back in that same form. Lauren, what happened with Toys R Us is perhaps one of

371
00:29:50.100 --> 00:29:54.620
<v Guest>the better known examples of a big trend in retail and private equity from the past couple decades.

372
00:29:54.620 --> 00:30:00.340
<v Guest>decades. Can you place the Toys R Us story in context for us? You know, I felt like my past,

373
00:30:00.980 --> 00:30:05.520
<v Guest>there are two years in which I felt like every day was just, you know, covering another retailer

374
00:30:05.520 --> 00:30:10.100
<v Guest>that had been acquired by a private equity firm and went bankrupt, whether that's Neiman Marcus,

375
00:30:10.460 --> 00:30:16.400
<v Guest>J.Crew, Payless, Toys R Us. And they're all different shades, but it's all really the same

376
00:30:16.400 --> 00:30:21.600
<v Guest>story where a private equity firm saw a company maybe was publicly traded, you know, trading on

377
00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:28.360
<v Guest>on the cheap. They, you know, saw gold and then everyone realized, oh, no, like this is this isn't

378
00:30:28.360 --> 00:30:33.540
<v Guest>working. Like, what do we do? Retail has changed and now we own these retailers. You know, many

379
00:30:33.540 --> 00:30:38.800
<v Guest>private equity firms like refuse to acquire a retailer now. They're too scarred by that experience.

380
00:30:39.300 --> 00:30:45.300
<v Guest>The lesson, frankly, of, you know, the run of leveraged buyouts onto these companies is be

381
00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:51.080
<v Guest>careful, understand your business, understand the risks, understand that like this is only going to

382
00:30:51.600 --> 00:30:55.160
<v Guest>if everything stays the same, then that rarely happens.

383
00:30:55.640 --> 00:31:01.520
<v Guest>You mentioned that a lot of people blamed you and your story for supposedly killing Toys R Us,

384
00:31:01.660 --> 00:31:05.000
<v Guest>but I wonder if you have a view on who or what actually did.

385
00:31:05.260 --> 00:31:10.920
<v Guest>Was it private equity ownership or no longer having a figure like Charles Lazarus at the helm?

386
00:31:11.080 --> 00:31:14.460
<v Guest>Was it some other company or even macroeconomic force?

387
00:31:14.840 --> 00:31:15.780
<v Guest>I think Walmart.

388
00:31:16.200 --> 00:31:20.060
<v Guest>I mean, everyone talks about Amazon and e-commerce and yes,

389
00:31:21.600 --> 00:31:26.920
<v Guest>It became really hard for specialty retailers to compete against people like Walmart who could compete on price.

390
00:31:27.160 --> 00:31:29.980
<v Guest>And I'm not saying that that makes Walmart evil.

391
00:31:30.200 --> 00:31:32.420
<v Guest>I just I think it changed the retail landscape.

392
00:31:33.260 --> 00:31:34.440
<v Guest>Of course, that's simplistic.

393
00:31:34.560 --> 00:31:36.480
<v Guest>There were other factors, you know, like the people.

394
00:31:37.820 --> 00:31:41.780
<v Guest>I think the loss of trust they had from their suppliers was a really big problem.

395
00:31:41.880 --> 00:31:47.260
<v Guest>And if toy companies could have continued to ship to Toys R Us, like that would have made a difference.

396
00:31:47.260 --> 00:31:53.400
<v Guest>But those things became so important because Walmart, you know, and big box retail disrupted the landscape.

397
00:31:53.620 --> 00:31:59.960
<v Guest>So I do think that sometimes people focus a little too much in Amazon and they forget the original disruptor, which was Walmart.

398
00:32:00.360 --> 00:32:04.060
<v Guest>And just to close, what happened to Charles Lazarus?

399
00:32:04.500 --> 00:32:12.180
<v Guest>Well, the craziest thing about all of this is Lazarus, like shortly after Toys R Us liquidated, died.

400
00:32:14.440 --> 00:32:16.900
<v Guest>He was an old man at that point.

401
00:32:17.260 --> 00:32:21.120
<v Guest>But you talk about kind of like the tragedy that this story seems to have.

402
00:32:21.320 --> 00:32:23.100
<v Guest>And, you know, it's a leveraged buyout gone bad.

403
00:32:23.540 --> 00:32:28.620
<v Guest>That doesn't sound inherently tragic, but it was just very sad.

404
00:32:28.780 --> 00:32:29.960
<v Guest>And it felt very poetic.

405
00:32:30.380 --> 00:32:33.740
<v Guest>Felt a little bit like there's something going on in the universe right now.

406
